// Essay reasoning: questions that coach the thinking behind the 12 tarot essay plans (built 20 Nov 2026).
// sp: spread id in spreads.js. k: the skill (tension, thesis, structure, proof, counter, land, trap).
// Every answer comes from the plan, its model essay or the v12 cards. Wrong options are tempting moves that lose marks.
window.REASON_SKILLS = {
  tension: 'Find the real question',
  thesis: 'Choose a thesis',
  structure: 'Order the argument',
  proof: 'Prove the point',
  counter: 'Meet the counter',
  land: 'Land the conclusion',
  trap: 'Avoid the trap'
};
window.REASON = [
// ---------- Smith v Fonterra and the 2026 Act ----------
{id:"sm1",sp:"smith",k:"tension",q:"The prompt asks you to assess the 2026 Act against three principles. What tension drives the whole essay?",a:"The Act is valid law, but it strains the separation of powers and the rule of law",w:["Whether the Supreme Court was right to send Mr Smith's claim to trial before the Act passed","Whether tort law is the right tool for climate harm at all","Whether tikanga ranks above statute in the common law"],x:["That's the merits of Smith. The prompt asks you to judge the Act against three principles, not to re-argue the Supreme Court.", "A policy debate. The question is constitutional: what the Act does to supremacy, separation and the rule of law.", "Tikanga is part of Smith's background, but the Act is valid whatever tikanga's rank. It isn't the tension the prompt sets."],why:"Validity is settled by sovereignty; the marks are in weighing the constitutional cost. The merits of Smith's claim are not the question."},
{id:"sm2",sp:"smith",k:"thesis",q:"Which thesis answers the prompt best?",a:"Valid law that strains separation of powers and the rule of law; the only real limits on Parliament here are political",w:["Invalid, because Parliament cannot end a case the Supreme Court has already sent to trial without a court's consent", "Unobjectionable: a sovereign Parliament may change any law, so the principles are not engaged", "Inconsistent with NZBORA's fair trial rights, so a court will decline to apply it to Mr Smith"],x:["Parliament needs no court's consent. Constitution Act s 15(1) and NZBORA s 4 keep the Act valid.", "It skips the question. The prompt asks you to weigh the cost to the separation of powers and the rule of law, so you can't call them 'not engaged'.", "NZBORA s 4 stops a court declining to apply an Act, however inconsistent it is."],why:"NZBORA s 4 stops any court declining to apply it, so 'invalid' is wrong. 'Unobjectionable' skips the two principles the question asks you to weigh."},
{id:"sm3",sp:"smith",k:"structure",q:"Why does the plan take sovereignty first?",a:"It settles validity, so later paragraphs can weigh cost, not legality",w:["It is the weakest argument, so it is best raised and dismissed before the stronger ones","Examiners give the first paragraph the most marks","The separation of powers point depends on it being proved invalid"],x:["Sovereignty isn't weak; it decides validity. You concede it, then use it as the base for the rest.", "There's no such marking rule. The order is about logic: legality first, then legitimacy.", "The point works because the Act is valid: a valid law can still strain the separation of powers."],why:"Legality first, legitimacy second: once you have conceded validity (s 15(1), s 4, Westco), every later paragraph is about cost."},
{id:"sm4",sp:"smith",k:"proof",q:"Your separation of powers paragraph says Parliament decided a pending case. Which authority shows that is a recognised constitutional wrong?",a:"The convention against legislative judgments, breached by the Clyde Dam Empowering Act 1982",w:["Westco Lagan: courts may rule on mandatory manner and form, so a pending case is protected too","Bill of Rights 1688 art 9: proceedings in Parliament cannot be questioned","Fitzgerald v Muldoon: the executive cannot suspend a law by announcement"],x:["Westco protects Parliament's process from courts; it says nothing about Parliament deciding a pending case.", "Article 9 stops courts questioning Parliament. It cuts the other way here.", "Fitzgerald limits the executive acting without Parliament. Here Parliament itself acted."],why:"The Clyde Dam Act is the standard example of Parliament overriding a pending legal process. Westco and art 9 protect Parliament; Fitzgerald limits the executive."},
{id:"sm5",sp:"smith",k:"proof",q:"To show that changing the law in the middle of a live claim offends the rule of law, which authority is the best analogy?",a:"Pora: Parliament must speak plainly before it imposes a heavier penalty retrospectively",w:["Payn: officers need an express power to stay on private land, so Parliament must speak expressly too","Hansen: a reverse onus limits the presumption of innocence","Taylor: a senior court may declare an Act inconsistent with NZBORA"],x:["Payn is about officers needing express powers, not about retrospectivity. The analogy is weak.", "Hansen is about reverse onus and s 5 justification, not changing the law mid-case.", "Taylor is about declarations of inconsistency, not retrospective legislation."],why:"Pora is the principle of legality applied to retrospectivity. Use it by analogy: the 2026 Act reaches back into proceedings already before the courts."},
{id:"sm6",sp:"smith",k:"proof",q:"Which earlier episode lets you say 2026 is a pattern, not a one-off?",a:"The 2013 Act after Atkinson, which restricted family-carer payments and barred new complaints",w:["The Parliament Act 2025, which restated privilege and reversed an earlier court decision about Hansard","The Legislation Act 2019, which replaced the Interpretation Act after Moonen","The Habeas Corpus Act 2001, passed after Noort"],x:["The Parliament Act 2025 restated privilege; it didn't shut down a claimant's case.", "The Legislation Act 2019 is a general interpretation statute, not a response to a court loss.", "Not an example of Parliament closing off a pending claim after a court ruling."],why:"The 2013 Act was passed under urgency to shut off litigation after a court loss, the closest precedent for the 2026 Act."},
{id:"sm7",sp:"smith",k:"counter",q:"What is the strongest counter to your criticism of the Act?",a:"Climate change is polycentric, and Parliament already has a scheme: ETS, budgets, the Commission",w:["Mr Smith can still sue the emitters overseas, so no one has really lost access to a court at all", "The Supreme Court's decision was only obiter, so Parliament overrode no actual ruling", "Tikanga cannot inform tort law after Takamore, so the claim was always going to fail"],x:["It concedes nothing about the constitution and isn't the Government's argument. Access to New Zealand courts was what was removed.", "Wrong in law: the Supreme Court's decision that the claim could go to trial was a ruling, not obiter.", "Wrong: Takamore treated tikanga as a relevant consideration, and Smith's tikanga argument was left for trial."],why:"Deference (card 3.2) and the Climate Change Response Act 2002 scheme give the Government's best reason. The other options are wrong in law or fact."},
{id:"sm8",sp:"smith",k:"land",q:"Which conclusion lands best?",a:"Without entrenched limits, these principles rest on Parliament's restraint",w:["The courts should refuse to apply the Act when Smith returns to trial, as the rule of law requires","The Act proves tikanga has no future in New Zealand tort law","The Governor-General should have refused royal assent"],x:["Courts can't refuse: NZBORA s 4 and sovereignty. A conclusion must be legally possible.", "Overclaims. The Act bars climate tort claims; it doesn't decide tikanga's place in tort generally.", "Royal assent follows ministerial advice by convention; refusing it would be a constitutional crisis, not a remedy."],why:"It follows from your thesis and opens the written-constitution point (the 1985 White Paper). The others are wrong in law or overclaim."},
{id:"sm9",sp:"smith",k:"trap",q:"Which sentence would cost you marks?",a:"'The Supreme Court held that Mr Smith's claims would succeed.'",w:["'Section 15(1) of the Constitution Act 1986 gives Parliament full power to make laws.'","'The Act applies the tort bar to pending proceedings, including Mr Smith's.'","'Section 4 of NZBORA stops a court declining to apply an Act.'"],x:["Accurate. This sentence wouldn't cost marks.", "Accurate: this is what the Act does.", "Accurate statement of NZBORA s 4."],why:"The Supreme Court held only that the claims were not clearly untenable and could go to trial."},

// ---------- The 2026 Treaty clause review ----------
{id:"tc1",sp:"treaty-clauses",k:"tension",q:"What is the real question underneath 'who should define the principles'?",a:"Legality v legitimacy: Parliament can set the principles' reach, but should one partner redefine a bilateral agreement alone?",w:["Whether the Treaty binds New Zealand in international law, and so limits a sovereign Parliament from outside the country", "Whether the Tribunal's findings bind the Crown, so Wai 3565 already decides the matter", "Whether the Treaty Principles Bill should be revived to state the principles in statute"],x:["International law status isn't the issue; the clauses are domestic statute.", "The Tribunal's findings are mostly recommendations, so Wai 3565 can't settle it.", "The Principles Bill was voted down; reviving it isn't the question asked."],why:"The model thesis separates legal power (Parliament) from the better claim to say what the principles mean (courts and Tribunal)."},
{id:"tc2",sp:"treaty-clauses",k:"trap",q:"How should you describe the Treaty clause changes in your essay?",a:"Proposed: three Bills before the Justice Committee, not yet law",w:["Enacted on 15 May 2026, when the Government announced the changes to all 19 Acts","Made by Cabinet decision on 23 February 2026","Struck down by the Waitangi Tribunal in Wai 3565"],x:["An announcement doesn't change an Act. Only Parliament can, after three readings and assent.", "Cabinet decides policy; it can't amend legislation.", "The Tribunal can't strike anything down. Wai 3565 found a breach of principles, which is a recommendation."],why:"A Cabinet decision and an announcement do not change an Act. Write 'would', not 'has'."},
{id:"tc3",sp:"treaty-clauses",k:"proof",q:"Which authority best shows that the principles bind only through statute, so Parliament already decides when they apply?",a:"Hoani Te Heuheu Tukino v Aotea District Maori Land Board (PC 1941)",w:["New Zealand Maori Council v Attorney-General (CA 1987)","Trans-Tasman Resources v Taranaki-Whanganui Conservation Board (SC 2021)","Ngāti Whātua Ōrākei Trust v Attorney-General (SC 2018)"],x:["The Lands case gave the principles content, but only because SOE Act s 9 made them law. It supports the courts' claim.", "Trans-Tasman read a Treaty clause generously. It's the courts' claim, not Parliament's.", "NWO 2018 is about review of settlement decisions, not the source of the principles' force."],why:"Te Heuheu: Treaty rights are unenforceable unless incorporated by statute. The Lands case and Trans-Tasman support the courts' claim, not Parliament's."},
{id:"tc4",sp:"treaty-clauses",k:"proof",q:"What is the courts' strongest claim to define the principles?",a:"They gave the principles their content: partnership, good faith, active protection and redress (Lands case)",w:["They can strike down Acts that breach the principles, as the Lands case did with the State-Owned Enterprises Act", "They have exclusive authority to interpret both Treaty texts under the 1975 Act", "They can turn the Tribunal's recommendations into binding orders against the Crown"],x:["Courts can't strike down Acts. The Lands case interpreted s 9; it didn't invalidate the SOE Act.", "That's the Tribunal's role under s 5(2) of the 1975 Act, not the courts'.", "Courts don't do that. The Tribunal's few binding powers (over certain Crown forest and SOE land) come from statute."],why:"Courts cannot strike down Acts (NZBORA s 4; sovereignty). Exclusive authority over the two texts belongs to the Tribunal (s 5(2))."},
{id:"tc5",sp:"treaty-clauses",k:"proof",q:"What gives the Waitangi Tribunal its claim?",a:"Treaty of Waitangi Act 1975 s 5(2): exclusive authority to determine the meaning and effect of both texts",w:["Section 6 of the 1975 Act makes its findings binding on the Crown in most claims, unlike a court's declaration", "It may review a Bill's Treaty compliance before the third reading in the House", "It is a court of record under the Senior Courts Act 2016, ranking with the High Court"],x:["Section 6 lets it inquire and recommend; most findings don't bind the Crown.", "It can report on Bills referred to it by the House, but it can't review or stop them.", "It's a commission of inquiry, not a court of record under the Senior Courts Act."],why:"Its findings are mostly recommendations, as Wai 3565 shows: the Government heard the review breached the principles and continued."},
{id:"tc6",sp:"treaty-clauses",k:"proof",q:"Why does capping clauses at 'take into account' matter?",a:"It turns a substantive duty ('give effect to') into one factor to weigh",w:["It removes the Tribunal's jurisdiction to hear claims under s 6 about any of the 19 Acts","It entrenches the principles so a later Parliament cannot repeal them","It makes breach of the principles actionable in tort"],x:["The Bills target clause wording in 19 Acts; they don't remove the Tribunal's jurisdiction.", "The opposite: the cap weakens the duty, and nothing is entrenched.", "No clause makes Treaty breach a tort; the change is to the strength of a statutory duty."],why:"Clause strength: 'give effect to' ≈ 'not inconsistent with' > 'take into account'. The cap would reverse the line from the Lands case to Trans-Tasman."},
{id:"tc7",sp:"treaty-clauses",k:"counter",q:"What is the best argument for Parliament specifying what the clauses mean?",a:"Rule of law: open-textured clauses built up case by case are hard to predict (Bingham: clear and predictable)",w:["Sovereignty: courts may not interpret Treaty clauses at all, so only Parliament can give them any meaning", "The Treaty Principles Bill passed in 2025, so the principles are already settled in statute", "The Tribunal endorsed the review in Wai 3565, giving it partnership legitimacy"],x:["Courts must interpret whatever Parliament enacts. Sovereignty decides who makes law, not who reads it.", "False: the Treaty Principles Bill was voted down on 10 April 2025.", "False: Wai 3565 found the review breached Treaty principles."],why:"Courts must interpret whatever Parliament enacts; the Treaty Principles Bill was voted down on 10 April 2025; Wai 3565 found a breach."},
{id:"tc8",sp:"treaty-clauses",k:"land",q:"Which conclusion lands best?",a:"Shared: Parliament sets which clauses exist; courts and Tribunal say what they require",w:["Courts should refuse to apply the new clauses if enacted, because the Treaty is bilateral", "Abolish the principles altogether, because judges invented them without any mandate", "Parliament should have no role, because the Treaty predates Parliament and outranks it"],x:["Courts can't refuse to apply an Act (sovereignty). A conclusion must work within the law.", "Overclaims and ignores that Parliament put the principles in statute.", "The Treaty doesn't outrank statute in New Zealand law (Te Heuheu)."],why:"It accepts the legal reality and still answers 'who should'. Then point to the unsettled place of the Treaty (1985 White Paper)."},

// ---------- Comity and judicial independence ----------
{id:"co1",sp:"comity",k:"tension",q:"Define comity in one line for your introduction.",a:"Mutual respect and restraint between the branches: a convention, not law",w:["The duty of lower courts to follow higher courts, so the law stays consistent across the system","The courtesy New Zealand courts show to foreign courts and judgments","Parliament's legal privilege against court interference under art 9"],x:["That's precedent (stare decisis), not comity.", "That's international comity, a different idea.", "Article 9 is a rule of law and only one strand. Comity is the wider convention of mutual restraint."],why:"Article 9 is law and only one strand. Comity is the wider convention. International comity is a different idea."},
{id:"co2",sp:"comity",k:"thesis",q:"Which thesis answers 'why does comity matter'?",a:"It lets the three principles coexist, but its force is only political",w:["Comity is enforceable by judicial review, so it protects the courts against political attack","Comity binds only politicians, so the Aitken panel went too far","Comity no longer matters since the Parliament Act 2025"],x:["Comity is a convention; courts don't enforce it by review.", "Comity binds every branch, and the panel found a judge in breach.", "Nothing in the Parliament Act 2025 ended comity; it restated privilege."],why:"The three principles pull against each other; comity is how they live together, which is also why its weakness matters."},
{id:"co3",sp:"comity",k:"tension",q:"Why is the Aitken finding a good hook?",a:"Comity is usually discussed as a restraint on politicians; here a judge was found in serious breach",w:["It was the first time a New Zealand judge was removed from office for misbehaviour on the bench", "It held comity is a rule of law the courts can enforce against any branch", "It found that Parliament had breached comity by criticising the judge"],x:["No judge was removed. The panel found a breach of comity, not misbehaviour justifying removal.", "A conduct panel's finding doesn't make comity a rule of law.", "The finding was about the judge's conduct, not Parliament's."],why:"The panel found a serious breach of comity, not misbehaviour, so no removal."},
{id:"co4",sp:"comity",k:"proof",q:"Which pair best shows comity flowing from the courts to Parliament?",a:"Prebble (courts out of the House) and Westco ('a two-way street')",w:["Taylor (declaration) and Make It 16 (second declaration of inconsistency)", "Fitzgerald v Muldoon (no suspending) and Payn (express powers)", "GCHQ (prerogative review) and Zaoui (security still reviewable)"],x:["These show courts speaking to Parliament through declarations: dialogue, not restraint.", "Both limit the executive, not the courts' stance toward Parliament.", "Review cases about the executive and the prerogative, not courts respecting Parliament."],why:"Taylor and Make It 16 show courts speaking to Parliament; Fitzgerald and Payn limit the executive; GCHQ and Zaoui are review cases."},
{id:"co5",sp:"comity",k:"proof",q:"What legally protects judges from the political branches?",a:"Constitution Act 1986 ss 23–24: removal only on an address of the House; salary cannot be cut",w:["Senior Courts Act 2016 s 74: the Supreme Court's leave criteria protect judges from political appeals", "NZBORA s 27(1): judges are entitled to natural justice before removal", "The Cabinet Manual 2023, which makes the comity convention binding law"],x:["Section 74 is about leave to appeal, not judges' tenure.", "Section 27(1) gives natural justice to people affected by decisions; it isn't tenure protection.", "The Cabinet Manual records conventions; it doesn't make them law."],why:"The Cabinet Manual records conventions; it does not make them law."},
{id:"co6",sp:"comity",k:"proof",q:"How does Taylor and the 2025 prisoner ban fit a comity essay?",a:"Disagreement within the law: court declares, Government responds, Parliament decides",w:["It shows the courts can now override Parliament on rights, by making declarations of inconsistency", "It was a breach of comity by the Supreme Court, which Parliament rightly answered", "It shows a declaration binds Parliament to amend the Act within a fixed time"],x:["A declaration doesn't override anything. The Act stays valid (s 4).", "Declarations are within the courts' role (Taylor), not a breach of comity.", "No fixed-time duty: ss 7A–7B require a response, not an amendment."],why:"Dialogue, not combat: each branch acts in its own sphere."},
{id:"co7",sp:"comity",k:"counter",q:"What is comity's main weakness?",a:"It is only a convention: courts recognise it but won't enforce it, so breach has only political consequences",w:["It has too few precedents, so it fails the Jennings test and is not even a convention, only a courtesy", "It applies only to the Supreme Court, so lower courts are not bound by it", "The Cabinet Manual 2023 made it law, so breaches are now actionable in court"],x:["Comity has plenty of precedents and passes the Jennings test.", "It applies to all courts and to the other branches.", "The Cabinet Manual doesn't make conventions law."],why:"On the Jennings test comity qualifies as a convention. That is exactly why it cannot be enforced."},
{id:"co8",sp:"comity",k:"land",q:"What asymmetry can your conclusion point out?",a:"Judges face a statutory conduct process; ministers and MPs who breach comity answer only politically",w:["MPs who breach comity can be disciplined by the Judicial Conduct Commissioner, just as judges can be", "Judges cannot be disciplined at all, because ss 23–24 give complete immunity", "Parliament may remove any judge by a simple vote, without misbehaviour or incapacity"],x:["The Judicial Conduct Commissioner deals only with judges, not MPs.", "Judges can be disciplined: there's a statutory conduct process, and removal by address for misbehaviour.", "Removal needs an address of the House for misbehaviour or incapacity (s 23)."],why:"Judicial Conduct Commissioner and Judicial Conduct Panel Act 2004 for judges; nothing equivalent for politicians. Section 23 requires misbehaviour or incapacity."},

// ---------- A written constitution ----------
{id:"wc1",sp:"written-constitution",k:"trap",q:"Which opening sentence would cost marks?",a:"'New Zealand has no written constitution.'",w:["'New Zealand's constitution is drawn from many sources.'","'Almost all of it can be changed by a simple majority.'","'The Constitution Act 1986 is an ordinary statute.'"],x:["Accurate; this wouldn't cost marks.", "Accurate: only a few provisions are entrenched.", "Accurate: it isn't supreme law."],why:"It is largely written but scattered and uncodified. Say 'uncodified', not 'unwritten'."},
{id:"wc2",sp:"written-constitution",k:"tension",q:"What does the case finally turn on?",a:"Whom to trust with power: voters or judges",w:["Whether the Treaty of Waitangi is valid law", "Whether MMP should be kept or replaced", "Whether the Cabinet Manual should become an Act"],x:["The Treaty's validity isn't the debate.", "The electoral system isn't the question.", "Too narrow; it's one detail of a codification proposal."],why:"Every for and against point comes back to that trade."},
{id:"wc3",sp:"written-constitution",k:"proof",q:"Which set best shows the risk of a single-chamber Parliament?",a:"The 2026 Climate Act, the 2013 carer Act, the 2025 prisoner ban",w:["Prebble, Westco Lagan and Payn, where courts deferred to Parliament","The Constitution Act 1986 and the Electoral Act 1993","Ellis, Takamore and Te Weehi"],x:["These show courts deferring, not Parliament overriding courts or rights.", "These are the constitutional statutes themselves, not examples of the risk.", "These are tikanga cases, not examples of a simple majority overriding rights."],why:"Each is Parliament overriding a court or a right by simple majority."},
{id:"wc4",sp:"written-constitution",k:"proof",q:"What is actually entrenched today?",a:"A few electoral rules (s 268), singly",w:["NZBORA itself, by its own s 4 and s 6", "The Treaty of Waitangi, through s 268", "Nothing: s 268 is only a convention"],x:["NZBORA s 4 makes NZBORA weaker than other Acts. It isn't entrenched.", "The Treaty isn't among the reserved provisions in s 268(1).", "Section 268 is statute and is obeyed, though it is itself only singly entrenched."],why:"Term, districts, voting age, secret ballot. Section 268 is itself not reserved, so a two-step repeal is possible on the orthodox view."},
{id:"wc5",sp:"written-constitution",k:"counter",q:"What is the strongest 'against' argument from New Zealand's own history?",a:"The 1985 White Paper's supreme-law Bill of Rights was rejected: judges would decide politics",w:["The Privy Council held a written constitution unlawful for a former colony with a sovereign Parliament", "The Treaty of Waitangi forbids codifying the constitution without Māori consent", "Simpson v Attorney-General held that the constitution must stay unwritten"],x:["No such Privy Council holding.", "The Treaty says nothing about codification; that's an argument about process, not history.", "Simpson (1955) is about invalid writs, not a ban on codification."],why:"NZBORA became an ordinary statute instead."},
{id:"wc6",sp:"written-constitution",k:"proof",q:"Which examples show the present constitution working without courts?",a:"Confidence, collective responsibility, caretaker",w:["Manner and form, as in Westco Lagan and Trethowan", "The reserved provisions in s 268", "Declarations of inconsistency in Taylor"],x:["Manner and form is enforced, if at all, by courts, so it isn't 'without courts'.", "Reserved provisions are statute, with a possible court role.", "Declarations are court-made."],why:"These are conventions: the flexibility argument against codifying."},
{id:"wc7",sp:"written-constitution",k:"proof",q:"How does Make It 16 serve the 'against' side?",a:"A court can declare; Parliament and voters still decide the age",w:["It shows the Supreme Court can lower the voting age itself by declaration", "It shows the voting age is not a reserved provision", "It shows declarations require a written constitution"],x:["The Court can't change the voting age; it only declared inconsistency.", "The voting age is reserved under s 268, which is why this matters.", "Declarations exist without a written constitution (Taylor)."],why:"Under supreme law the court could have struck the age down; under NZBORA it can only speak."},
{id:"wc8",sp:"written-constitution",k:"land",q:"Which conclusion lands best?",a:"Codify in one text, entrench only the core; supremacy turns on trust",w:["Adopt US-style supreme law now, because Parliament can no longer be trusted","Change nothing, because nothing has gone wrong","An unentrenched constitution would still bind future Parliaments"],x:["One-sided and unconditional. Examiners reward a conclusion with a condition.", "Ignores the evidence (2013, 2025, 2026) you used in the essay.", "Wrong: under s 15(1) an unentrenched text binds no later Parliament."],why:"Conditional and honest about s 15(1): a constitution binds later Parliaments only if entrenched and respected."},

// ---------- Is judicial review worth it? ----------
{id:"jr1",sp:"review",k:"thesis",q:"Which thesis answers 'is review worth it'?",a:"Worth it where speed, rights or fair process matter; least worth it where the client wants a different merits result",w:["Always worth it, because the court corrects the decision and substitutes the right one for the claimant", "Never worth it, because every remedy is discretionary and costs are rarely recovered", "Worth it only if the claim reaches the Supreme Court and sets a precedent"],x:["Review doesn't substitute decisions; it tests lawfulness and usually sends the decision back.", "Too absolute. Christiansen and Auckland show review can be worth it.", "Value depends on the client's goal, not on reaching the top court."],why:"Review tests lawfulness, not merits. The court usually sends the decision back to be remade."},
{id:"jr2",sp:"review",k:"proof",q:"Which case is your best example of speed?",a:"Christiansen: an interim order within days under JRPA 2016 s 15",w:["Lab Tests: the contract was restored on appeal within weeks of the High Court ruling","Borrowdale: a declaration months after the lockdown","Zaoui: the security certificate went to the Supreme Court"],x:["Lab Tests lost in the end; it isn't a speed example.", "A declaration months later shows delay, not speed.", "Zaoui took years; security was reviewable, but slowly."],why:"He was released on conditions to visit his dying father: relief that mattered while it still could."},
{id:"jr3",sp:"review",k:"proof",q:"Why is University of Auckland v TEC your 'better process' example?",a:"The remedy matched the ground won: consult first",w:["The universities won on irrationality, so the ranking was never published","The ranking was suppressed permanently","It created a general duty to give reasons"],x:["They lost on irrationality and won on natural justice.", "TEC could publish after consulting.", "Auckland created no general duty to give reasons."],why:"They won on natural justice and legitimate expectation, lost on irrationality; TEC could still publish after consulting."},
{id:"jr4",sp:"review",k:"counter",q:"What is the strongest negative?",a:"Relief is discretionary; the decision is often just remade",w:["Review lies only against ministers, not officials or public bodies","The court substitutes its own decision on the merits","Review needs leave from the Court of Appeal"],x:["Review extends to public bodies and powers, not just ministers (JRPA 2016 s 5).", "The opposite: courts don't substitute their own decision.", "Claims start in the High Court; no Court of Appeal leave is needed."],why:"Set aside and reconsider is the usual result. Delay, futility and alternative remedies can defeat relief."},
{id:"jr5",sp:"review",k:"proof",q:"What does Borrowdale add to the negatives?",a:"Even a win can be only a declaration, and a finely balanced one",w:["The whole lockdown was unlawful from start to finish, so damages followed","Health Act orders cannot be reviewed","Interim relief is granted as a matter of course"],x:["Only nine days were unlawful, the s 70 orders were lawful, and no damages followed.", "Health Act orders were reviewed and found lawful.", "Interim relief is discretionary, and none was given in Borrowdale."],why:"Nine days unlawful (not prescribed by law); the s 70 orders were lawful; relief was nearly refused."},
{id:"jr6",sp:"review",k:"structure",q:"Why does intensity of review belong in the 'when is it worth it' paragraph?",a:"Courts defer on security, policy and resources, and look harder where rights or individual fairness are at stake",w:["Intensity only matters in criminal appeals, not in review of administrative decisions by officials or ministers", "Every ground is applied with the same intensity, whatever the subject matter", "Deference means the decision cannot be reviewed at all, so it ends the claim"],x:["Intensity is a review concept, not about criminal appeals.", "Intensity varies with context; that's the whole point.", "Deference changes how closely courts look, not whether they can (Zaoui)."],why:"Deference goes to how closely, not whether (Zaoui: security is still reviewable)."},
{id:"jr7",sp:"review",k:"proof",q:"What does Lab Tests teach a client?",a:"Reviewable is not winning: public in substance, but the claim lost",w:["Commercial decisions by public bodies are never reviewable, whatever their source", "A past conflict of interest always voids a public contract", "Probity is the standard for every public decision"],x:["Lab Tests held the contract reviewable because it was public in substance.", "The conflict didn't void the contract; the claim failed.", "The court said the standard depends on the body, function, context and wrong."],why:"The Court of Appeal said the standard depends on the body, function, context and alleged wrong."},
{id:"jr8",sp:"review",k:"land",q:"Which cheaper route does your conclusion offer?",a:"An Ombudsman: wider grounds ('unjust', 'wrong'), but only a recommendation (Ombudsmen Act ss 13, 22)",w:["The Waitangi Tribunal, which can make binding orders in most claims against any government department", "The Human Rights Review Tribunal, which hears any complaint about a public decision", "A Declaratory Judgments Act claim, which avoids court fees and costs entirely"],x:["The Tribunal deals with Treaty claims and mostly recommends.", "The HRRT handles discrimination and privacy matters, not every public decision.", "Court fees and costs still apply under the Declaratory Judgments Act."],why:"Advise the client to treat review as one tool among several."},

// ---------- Prisoner voting 2025 ----------
{id:"pv1",sp:"prisoner-vote",k:"thesis",q:"Which thesis answers 'how far does the Bill of Rights restrain Parliament'?",a:"No legal restraint (s 4), but Parliament must choose openly, after warning",w:["Fully: a court can strike down the 2025 Act once a declaration has been made","Not at all: NZBORA has no effect on Parliament","The 2025 Act is invalid because Taylor declared the 2010 ban inconsistent"],x:["Courts can't strike down Acts; s 4 keeps the 2025 Act valid.", "Too far: s 7 reports, s 6 readings and declarations all bear on Parliament's choices.", "A declaration doesn't invalidate the Act, and the 2025 Act is new law."],why:"The model thesis: no legal restraint, real political discipline."},
{id:"pv2",sp:"prisoner-vote",k:"proof",q:"Why could a simple majority reinstate the ban?",a:"Electoral Act s 80 (prisoners) is not reserved under s 268",w:["Section 268 does not apply to amendment Acts, only to new Acts", "The 2025 Act was passed by more than 75% of the House", "Prisoners fall within the reserved voting-age provision"],x:["Section 268 protects the reserved provisions however they're amended.", "The margin doesn't matter: no reserved provision was involved, so a simple majority was enough.", "The voting-age provision is about age (18), not prisoners."],why:"Only the term, districts, voting age and secret ballot are reserved."},
{id:"pv3",sp:"prisoner-vote",k:"structure",q:"In Hansen's method, where does s 4 come in?",a:"Last: only if s 6 cannot give a consistent meaning",w:["First, before the court identifies Parliament's intended meaning", "Before s 5, to decide whether a right is engaged", "Only where the Attorney-General made a s 7 report"],x:["Meaning is identified first; s 4 comes at the end.", "Section 4 has nothing to do with whether a right is engaged.", "Section 4 applies whether or not there was a s 7 report."],why:"Meaning → limit? → justified (s 5)? → consistent meaning (s 6)? → if none, apply the Act (s 4)."},
{id:"pv4",sp:"prisoner-vote",k:"proof",q:"What is a s 7 report, and what did it do in 2025?",a:"The Attorney-General's warning that a Bill appears inconsistent; a warning, not a veto, and Parliament passed the Bill anyway",w:["A court's declaration of inconsistency, which suspended the Bill until Parliament voted on it again after the election", "A select committee veto, which the Government overrode by taking the Bill under urgency", "The Governor-General's reserve power to refuse assent, which was not used in 2025"],x:["That's a declaration, which comes from a court after enactment. A s 7 report comes from the Attorney-General before.", "Select committees can't veto Bills.", "The Governor-General acts on advice; s 7 isn't a reserve power."],why:"The 2025 report found parts inconsistent with s 12 (news reports)."},
{id:"pv5",sp:"prisoner-vote",k:"proof",q:"Why does a declaration respect s 4 and still matter?",a:"The Act stays valid, but ss 7A–7B make the Government respond in public",w:["It suspends the Act until Parliament votes again on the declared provision","It entitles prisoners to compensation","It requires Parliament to amend the Act within six months"],x:["A declaration doesn't suspend the Act (s 4).", "No compensation follows from a declaration.", "No deadline to amend; only a duty to respond."],why:"Attorney-General presents it to the House within 6 sitting days; the Government must respond."},
{id:"pv6",sp:"prisoner-vote",k:"tension",q:"Why is the restraint weakest on the franchise?",a:"Those who lose the vote can't vote out who removed it",w:["The right to vote is not protected by NZBORA", "Courts never hear cases about voting rights", "Section 12 of NZBORA is subject to Electoral Act s 268"],x:["The right to vote is protected by NZBORA s 12.", "Courts did hear the cases: Taylor and Make It 16.", "Section 12 isn't subject to s 268; s 268 protects other electoral rules."],why:"The political check fails exactly where the political remedy is removed."},
{id:"pv7",sp:"prisoner-vote",k:"counter",q:"What is the respectable defence of the system?",a:"Dialogue: courts identify, the A-G warns, elected members decide",w:["Courts already have the last word, because declarations must be obeyed", "Prisoners can still vote on the Māori roll, so the right is not lost", "Section 7 reports bind the House, so Parliament acted unlawfully"],x:["Declarations don't have to be obeyed; the Act stays valid.", "Disqualification applies to all prisoners on either roll.", "Section 7 reports are warnings; ignoring one is lawful."],why:"Taylor (CA): government is a 'collaborative enterprise', each branch in its sphere."},
{id:"pv8",sp:"prisoner-vote",k:"land",q:"Which conclusion lands best?",a:"A legal restraint needs supreme law, which was rejected in 1985",w:["Courts should now refuse to apply the 2025 Act, because Taylor already declared the ban", "The Attorney-General should have vetoed the Bill under s 7", "The Act will lapse because of the earlier declaration"],x:["Courts can't refuse to apply the Act, and Taylor concerned the 2010 ban.", "The Attorney-General has no veto under s 7.", "Declarations don't make Acts lapse."],why:"Until then NZBORA is an interpretive guide and a public alarm bell."},

// ---------- After the 2026 election ----------
{id:"el1",sp:"election",k:"tension",q:"What is the key point about the rules for the post-election period?",a:"Almost none are law: they are conventions recorded in the Cabinet Manual",w:["The Constitution Act 1986 sets out the caretaker rules in detail, as amended in 2026","The Electoral Commission runs the executive until results","The Governor-General governs personally until a PM is chosen"],x:["The Constitution Act doesn't contain the caretaker rules; the 2026 amendment was narrow.", "The Electoral Commission runs elections, not government.", "Ministers continue in office; the Governor-General doesn't govern personally."],why:"Parliament has codified only narrow points, such as the 2026 amendment to s 6."},
{id:"el2",sp:"election",k:"proof",q:"With an unclear result, what does the caretaker convention require?",a:"Routine business; defer big decisions; consult if one can't wait",w:["Make no decisions of any kind until the official results are declared", "Act on the wishes of the party that won the most votes", "Get the Governor-General's approval for every decision made"],x:["Routine government continues; the convention isn't a full freeze.", "The convention looks to the likely government, which isn't always the party with the most votes under MMP.", "The Governor-General doesn't approve ministers' ordinary decisions."],why:"If the result is clear, act on the incoming government's wishes on urgent matters."},
{id:"el3",sp:"election",k:"proof",q:"Who becomes Prime Minister under MMP?",a:"Whoever commands the confidence of the House",w:["The leader of the party with the most votes nationally", "Whoever the Governor-General personally judges best", "The leader of the largest party, automatically"],x:["Under MMP the largest party may not command confidence.", "The Governor-General appoints on advice, not on personal judgment.", "There's no automatic appointment; confidence decides."],why:"The Governor-General does not choose a government; personal discretion is a last resort in a crisis."},
{id:"el4",sp:"election",k:"proof",q:"What has Parliament actually codified for this period?",a:"The 2026 amendment to s 6: outgoing ministers stay until list MPs are declared",w:["The caretaker convention itself, which the 2026 amendment wrote into the Constitution Act", "The rule that the Prime Minister must command the confidence of the House", "The Governor-General's reserve powers, set out in the Letters Patent"],x:["The caretaker convention is still convention, recorded in the Cabinet Manual.", "Confidence remains a convention.", "The reserve powers aren't set out as rules in the Letters Patent."],why:"A targeted fix to s 6, not codification of the conventions."},
{id:"el5",sp:"election",k:"proof",q:"Why is supply tied to confidence?",a:"Constitution Act s 22: no spending without an Act of Parliament",w:["Supply is a convention, but the courts enforce it after an election", "Treasury may spend freely during an election until a government forms", "The Governor-General may authorise spending alone while talks go on"],x:["Supply rests on statute (s 22), and courts don't 'enforce supply'.", "Treasury needs parliamentary authority to spend.", "The Governor-General can't authorise spending without Parliament."],why:"Losing supply means losing the ability to govern."},
{id:"el6",sp:"election",k:"counter",q:"What is the strongest case FOR codifying?",a:"Certainty: a tight MMP result is when unwritten rules are most likely to be tested",w:["Courts must decide formation disputes, so they need a statute to apply to the parties", "Conventions are unlawful unless backed by statute", "The Cabinet Manual is unconstitutional as it stands"],x:["Courts don't decide government formation, and statute isn't needed for that reason.", "Conventions are lawful; they're just not enforced by courts.", "The Cabinet Manual is an accepted guide, not unconstitutional."],why:"With no authoritative text, the public cannot judge a Governor-General's personal decision."},
{id:"el7",sp:"election",k:"counter",q:"What is the strongest case AGAINST codifying?",a:"Conventions adapt; statutes harden and invite litigation",w:["Parliament has no power to codify constitutional conventions", "Codifying a convention would itself be unconstitutional", "Courts already enforce the conventions, so a statute adds nothing"],x:["Parliament can codify anything (s 15(1)).", "Codifying isn't unconstitutional.", "Courts recognise conventions but don't enforce them."],why:"GCHQ: some prerogatives (like appointing ministers) are unsuited to review."},
{id:"el8",sp:"election",k:"land",q:"Which conclusion lands best?",a:"Keep the core as convention, with targeted statutory fixes",w:["Codify every rule and make all of it enforceable in court", "Abolish the caretaker convention and let ministers govern as normal", "Let the Governor-General choose the next government personally"],x:["Making all of it court-enforceable invites the litigation the 'against' side warns of.", "The caretaker convention is what keeps the period orderly.", "Choosing personally is a last-resort reserve power, not a design."],why:"Matches what Parliament has done (s 6 fix) and keeps flexibility."},

// ---------- Tikanga as part of the fabric ----------
{id:"tk1",sp:"tikanga",k:"structure",q:"How should the tikanga essay be organised?",a:"Glazebrook J's three routes in turn, then Parliament's limit",w:["Chronologically, from 1840 to the present day, one era per paragraph", "One paragraph per case, in order of decision", "Tikanga values first, then how the law treats them"],x:["Chronology hides the routes the prompt names.", "A case list isn't an argument.", "The prompt asks how law and tikanga relate, so start with the routes."],why:"The prompt hands you the structure; use its own three words."},
{id:"tk2",sp:"tikanga",k:"tension",q:"Why does He Poutama's distinction matter?",a:"As evidence, tikanga is filtered; as law, it can shape the rules",w:["He Poutama held that tikanga outranks statute where they conflict", "He Poutama binds the courts, because it is a Law Commission report", "He Poutama says tikanga must be codified before courts use it"],x:["He Poutama doesn't say tikanga outranks statute.", "A Law Commission study paper isn't binding.", "He Poutama doesn't call for codification first."],why:"It is a Law Commission study paper: tikanga understood on its own terms."},
{id:"tk3",sp:"tikanga",k:"proof",q:"Which is the best example of tikanga as part of a legal test?",a:"Takutai Moana Act 2011 s 58: held in accordance with tikanga",w:["NZBORA s 20: the rights of minorities, including Māori, to enjoy their culture", "Legislation Act 2019 s 10: text read in light of purpose", "Senior Courts Act 2016 s 74: Treaty issues for leave"],x:["Section 20 protects minority culture; tikanga isn't the legal test there.", "Section 10 is about interpretation generally, not tikanga.", "Section 74 is about leave; it isn't a tikanga test."],why:"Tikanga is in the test itself, though the 2025 amendments make title harder to prove."},
{id:"tk4",sp:"tikanga",k:"proof",q:"What did Trans-Tasman add to the legislation strand?",a:"Tikanga is 'other applicable law'; its interests are 'existing interests'",w:["Tikanga gives iwi a veto over consents in their rohe under the EEZ Act", "Tikanga overrides the EEZ Act where they conflict", "Tikanga is irrelevant to environmental decision-making"],x:["Consideration isn't a veto.", "Tikanga sits within the Act's scheme; it doesn't override it.", "Trans-Tasman held the opposite."],why:"EEZ Act s 59(2)(l). Consideration is not a veto."},
{id:"tk5",sp:"tikanga",k:"proof",q:"How do Takamore and Ellis differ?",a:"Takamore: a relevant consideration; Ellis: part of developing the law",w:["Takamore made tikanga binding on executors; Ellis then rejected that approach", "Both decided where a deceased person should be buried", "Ellis concerned customary title; Takamore concerned fishing"],x:["Takamore didn't make tikanga binding; the executor won.", "Only Takamore was about burial; Ellis was about an appeal after death.", "Neither case was about title or fishing."],why:"From consideration (2012) to development of the law itself (2022)."},
{id:"tk6",sp:"tikanga",k:"proof",q:"Which examples fit the policy and practice strand?",a:"Te Ao Mārama, Cabinet Manual Treaty analysis, pūkenga evidence",w:["Ngāti Apa and Te Weehi: customary rights recognised at common law", "Takamore and Ellis: tikanga in the common law", "The Foreshore and Seabed Act 2004 and the 2026 tort bar"],x:["Those are common-law customary rights cases.", "Those are common-law cases.", "Those are limits on tikanga, not policy recognising it."],why:"Ngāti Apa and Te Weehi are common-law rights; the last pair belong in the limit paragraph."},
{id:"tk7",sp:"tikanga",k:"counter",q:"What is the limit paragraph's point?",a:"Parliament holds the scissors: it narrowed tikanga in 2004, 2025, 2026",w:["Courts can strike down laws that conflict with tikanga, as Ngāti Apa showed","The Treaty entrenches tikanga against repeal","Tikanga applies only between Māori"],x:["Courts can't strike down statutes; Ngāti Apa was reversed by statute.", "The Treaty doesn't entrench anything (Te Heuheu).", "Tikanga is recognised in the general law, not just between Māori."],why:"Ngāti Apa → Foreshore and Seabed Act 2004; 2025 marine title amendments; 2026 tort bar."},
{id:"tk8",sp:"tikanga",k:"land",q:"Which conclusion lands best?",a:"Largely true; whether it becomes the foundation depends on Parliament",w:["False: tikanga is only ever evidence of fact in the state courts, never law", "Entirely true: tikanga now overrides statute where they conflict", "Beside the point: Glazebrook J's remark was a dissent"],x:["Ellis recognises tikanga in developing the law, not just as evidence.", "Statute still prevails over tikanga.", "Glazebrook J was in the majority."],why:"All five judges accepted that tikanga has a place in developing the common law; Glazebrook J was in the majority."},

// ---------- Treaty partnership in 2026 ----------
{id:"pa1",sp:"partnership",k:"trap",q:"How should you use Cooke P's quote?",a:"Quote it with attribution (at 664), then say what it required in law",w:["Paraphrase it without quotation marks as your own opening idea, to save time", "Treat it as a holding that the Treaty is supreme law in New Zealand", "Attribute it to the Waitangi Tribunal's 1987 Lands report"],x:["Breaks the exam's quotation rule.", "The Lands case interpreted a statute; it didn't make the Treaty supreme.", "The quote is Cooke P's, from the Court of Appeal."],why:"The exam rule: reproduced text in quotation marks, sourced to its author."},
{id:"pa2",sp:"partnership",k:"thesis",q:"Which thesis answers 'what does partnership mean now, and who decides'?",a:"A duty only where statute imposes it; wider in Tribunal and tikanga; being narrowed",w:["A binding constitutional rule that the courts will enforce against Parliament if a clause is repealed", "A historical phrase from 1987 with no legal effect today", "Whatever the Waitangi Tribunal says, because its findings bind the Crown"],x:["Courts don't enforce partnership against Parliament.", "Partnership still has legal effect wherever statute imports it.", "Tribunal findings are mostly recommendations."],why:"Three meanings, and the answer to 'who decides' differs for each."},
{id:"pa3",sp:"partnership",k:"structure",q:"Why bring in the two texts early?",a:"The texts differ: sovereignty v kāwanatanga and tino rangatiratanga",w:["Because only the English text is legally binding in New Zealand courts", "Because the courts have chosen te Tiriti over the English text", "Because no chief signed the English text at Waitangi in 1840"],x:["Both texts matter; courts use the principles to reconcile them.", "Courts haven't chosen one text over the other.", "About 39 chiefs did sign the English text."],why:"About 39 chiefs signed the English text; most signed te Tiriti. Courts use the principles to avoid choosing."},
{id:"pa4",sp:"partnership",k:"proof",q:"Which authority shows statutory partnership can be substantive?",a:"Conservation Act s 4 'give effect to' (Ngāi Tai ki Tāmaki)",w:["A 'take into account' clause, as proposed in the 2026 clause Bills", "Hoani Te Heuheu Tukino (PC 1941)", "The Treaty Principles Bill 2024"],x:["'Take into account' is the weakest standard.", "Te Heuheu shows the Treaty needs statute to have force, not substantive partnership.", "The Principles Bill was defeated and would have narrowed partnership."],why:"Substantive, not merely procedural, attention to Māori interests."},
{id:"pa5",sp:"partnership",k:"proof",q:"What did the Tribunal find in He Whakaputanga me te Tiriti (Wai 1040)?",a:"The rangatira did not cede sovereignty; they agreed to share power",w:["Crown sovereignty was absolute and complete from February 1840 onwards", "The State-Owned Enterprises Act 1986 was invalid", "The Treaty has no legal or moral meaning today"],x:["That's the opposite of the Tribunal's finding.", "The Tribunal can't invalidate Acts.", "The Tribunal found meaning, not none."],why:"It is a Tribunal finding, so it informs the argument rather than binding the courts."},
{id:"pa6",sp:"partnership",k:"trap",q:"What is the status of Parliament's two moves to redefine partnership?",a:"Principles Bill voted down April 2025; clause Bills at select committee",w:["Both are now in force, after passing under urgency before the 2026 election", "The Principles Bill passed in 2025; the clause Bills were withdrawn", "Both were withdrawn before first reading after the Tribunal reported"],x:["Neither was enacted.", "The Principles Bill was voted down; the clause Bills are at select committee.", "Both reached first reading."],why:"Say 'would' for the clause Bills."},
{id:"pa7",sp:"partnership",k:"counter",q:"What is partnership's legal weakness?",a:"It is unprotected: Parliament can rewrite every clause by majority",w:["Courts have declared partnership unenforceable in law since the Lands case", "The Tribunal has abandoned partnership as a principle", "Partnership applies only to land claims, not other taonga"],x:["No court has declared partnership unenforceable.", "The Tribunal still relies on partnership.", "Partnership isn't limited to land."],why:"Te Heuheu plus s 15(1): force only through statute, and statute can change."},
{id:"pa8",sp:"partnership",k:"land",q:"Which conclusion lands best?",a:"It rests on political judgment until the Treaty's place is settled",w:["Partnership is secure, because the Lands case binds every later court", "Partnership ended when the 2026 Bills were introduced", "The courts will enforce partnership against Parliament if pressed"],x:["The Lands case binds only on the statute it interpreted, and statute can change.", "The Bills aren't law, and partnership survives elsewhere anyway.", "Courts can't enforce partnership against Parliament."],why:"The Lands case binds only on the statute it interpreted."},

// ---------- Unreasonableness ----------
{id:"un1",sp:"unreasonable",k:"tension",q:"Why does Joseph call unreasonableness problematic?",a:"It edges toward the merits, though review is not an appeal",w:["It is not recognised as a ground in New Zealand law since the 2016 Act", "It applies only to ministers' decisions", "It requires proof of bad faith by officials"],x:["Unreasonableness is a recognised ground, and the 2016 Act didn't remove it.", "It applies to any exercise of public power.", "Bad faith is a separate ground."],why:"Illegality has one right answer (the court's); procedure is the courts' home ground. Unreasonableness looks at outcome."},
{id:"un2",sp:"unreasonable",k:"trap",q:"How should you use Joseph's line?",a:"Quote it with attribution: Joseph at 1067",w:["Present it as Lord Diplock's holding in GCHQ", "Paraphrase it without credit as your own view", "Attribute it to the Court of Appeal in Wednesbury"],x:["GCHQ is Lord Diplock's taxonomy; the line is Joseph's.", "Taking an idea without credit breaches the exam rule.", "Wednesbury is an English case, and the line isn't from it."],why:"An idea from another writer needs acknowledgement even when paraphrased."},
{id:"un3",sp:"unreasonable",k:"proof",q:"Which case shows claimants rarely win on unreasonableness alone?",a:"University of Auckland v TEC: won on fairness, lost on irrationality",w:["Christiansen: won on unreasonableness, the only ground the court decided","GCHQ: national security made the decision irrational","Zaoui: the certificate was unreasonable"],x:["Christiansen won on error of law and a mandatory consideration; unreasonableness wasn't decided.", "GCHQ wasn't decided on irrationality; national security prevailed.", "Zaoui is about security and natural justice, not unreasonableness."],why:"Difficult, even flawed, but not impossible: so not irrational."},
{id:"un4",sp:"unreasonable",k:"proof",q:"What did Christiansen actually win on?",a:"Error of law and ignoring a mandatory consideration",w:["Unreasonableness: no reasonable official could refuse", "Apparent bias: officials had prejudged his request", "Legitimate expectation: he had been promised an exemption"],x:["Unreasonableness wasn't decided.", "There was no bias finding.", "There was no promise and no legitimate expectation claim."],why:"Illegality did the work, which supports Joseph's point."},
{id:"un5",sp:"unreasonable",k:"proof",q:"Where rights are engaged, what replaces bare unreasonableness?",a:"The structured s 5 test (Grounded Kiwis)",w:["Nothing: rights cases use Wednesbury unchanged", "Strict liability: any limit on a right is unlawful", "A test of bad faith by the decision-maker"],x:["Rights cases use the structured s 5 test.", "Limits can be justified under s 5.", "Bad faith isn't the test."],why:"The MIQ lottery failed minimal impairment."},
{id:"un6",sp:"unreasonable",k:"counter",q:"What may be even more problematic than unreasonableness?",a:"The variable standard of review itself (Lab Tests)",w:["Natural justice, because it has no settled content at all", "Illegality, because courts are poorly placed to decide law", "Remedies, because they are mandatory once a ground is made out"],x:["Natural justice has settled limbs: hearing, bias, legitimate expectation.", "Illegality is the courts' home ground.", "Remedies are discretionary."],why:"The answer to 'if not, which ground?' that the question invites."},
{id:"un7",sp:"unreasonable",k:"land",q:"Which conclusion lands best?",a:"Largely right, but keep it as a backstop and say when it applies",w:["Abolish unreasonableness, because it always invites merits review", "Replace all three grounds with proportionality straight away", "Unreasonableness is the least problematic ground, so Joseph is wrong"],x:["Too far: it leaves no backstop against absurd outcomes.", "Not current law, and it doesn't answer 'which ground is most problematic'.", "Contradicts the evidence you used: claims rarely win on it, and it edges into the merits."],why:"A review that could never reach an absurd result would not be worth much (NZBORA s 27(2))."},
{id:"un8",sp:"unreasonable",k:"structure",q:"How should the plan order the argument?",a:"Agree with Joseph, then argue the variable standard is worse",w:["Define each ground at length, then simply agree with Joseph's view", "Discuss remedies first, then the grounds in Diplock's order", "Argue for proportionality first, then define Wednesbury"],x:["Describing the grounds wastes time and doesn't answer 'which is worse'.", "Remedies aren't the question.", "Proportionality is a possible alternative, not the structure the question asks for."],why:"The question asks 'do you agree? if not, which is worse?', so answer both."},

// ---------- Māori representation ----------
{id:"mr1",sp:"maori-rep",k:"tension",q:"How do seats, wards and a Māori parliament differ in kind?",a:"Seats and wards give a voice inside; a parliament would stand apart",w:["All three are entrenched under Electoral Act s 268, so none can be abolished", "Seats work locally, wards work nationally", "Only a Māori parliament would need any legislation"],x:["None of the three is entrenched.", "Seats are national (Parliament); wards are local (councils).", "Seats and wards also exist by statute."],why:"That difference drives the separation of powers paragraph."},
{id:"mr2",sp:"maori-rep",k:"proof",q:"What is the legal status of the Māori seats?",a:"Not reserved under s 268: a simple majority could abolish them",w:["Entrenched by Electoral Act 1993 s 268, so abolition needs 75% or a poll","Protected by the Treaty of Waitangi Act 1975","Protected by NZBORA s 19, so courts could stop abolition"],x:["Māori seats aren't reserved under s 268.", "The 1975 Act doesn't protect them.", "Section 19 can't stop Parliament (s 4)."],why:"Section 45 provides for Māori electoral districts; it is not a reserved provision."},
{id:"mr3",sp:"maori-rep",k:"proof",q:"What did the 1986 Royal Commission on the Electoral System recommend?",a:"Abolish the seats if MMP came in",w:["Entrench the Māori seats under s 268", "Double the number of Māori seats", "Create a separate Māori parliament"],x:["It didn't recommend entrenchment.", "It didn't recommend more seats.", "No Māori parliament was proposed."],why:"Parliament kept the seats anyway."},
{id:"mr4",sp:"maori-rep",k:"proof",q:"How do the wards illustrate the rule of law?",a:"Councils had to obey the 2024 Act's binding polls",w:["Councils could ignore the 2024 Act if it breached the Treaty", "The courts struck the 2024 Act down for breaching NZBORA", "Wards are protected by s 268, so councils kept them"],x:["Councils must obey Acts; the Treaty isn't supreme law.", "No court struck the 2024 Act down.", "Wards aren't reserved; 24 of 42 councils dropped them."],why:"In 2025, 24 of 42 councils dropped their wards. The rule of law binds councils to Parliament's choice."},
{id:"mr5",sp:"maori-rep",k:"proof",q:"Why can't a Māori parliament escape supremacy?",a:"It would need an Act, and Parliament could repeal it (s 15(1))",w:["It could be created by Tribunal recommendation, without any Act of Parliament","The Treaty would entrench it automatically","Tino rangatiratanga would let it override Parliament in law"],x:["The Tribunal only recommends; it can't create institutions.", "The Treaty entrenches nothing.", "In law, Parliament remains supreme (s 15(1))."],why:"The argument for it rests on partnership and the Tribunal, not on existing law."},
{id:"mr6",sp:"maori-rep",k:"proof",q:"What does the Māori parliament proposal draw on?",a:"Matike Mai Aotearoa and Wai 1040",w:["The Lands case holding on partnership", "The Royal Commission's 1986 report", "Electoral Act 1993 s 268"],x:["The Lands case is about the principles, not a separate parliament.", "The Royal Commission recommended abolishing the seats.", "Section 268 is electoral entrenchment."],why:"Rangatiratanga sphere, kāwanatanga sphere, relational sphere."},
{id:"mr7",sp:"maori-rep",k:"counter",q:"What is the strongest case for Māori representation in all three forms?",a:"Partnership: one partner can abolish the other's voice by majority",w:["Electoral equality requires separate seats for every minority group in Parliament", "The rule of law requires councils to keep Māori wards", "NZBORA s 19 guarantees Māori separate representation"],x:["Electoral equality cuts against separate seats.", "The rule of law required councils to follow the 2024 Act, not to keep wards.", "Section 19 doesn't guarantee separate representation."],why:"Cooke P's partnership and the duty of active protection."},
{id:"mr8",sp:"maori-rep",k:"land",q:"Which conclusion lands best?",a:"All three last only as long as Parliament allows them",w:["The seats are safe because they have existed since 1867", "Councils will restore wards because the law now requires them", "A Māori parliament is unlawful in principle under s 15(1)"],x:["Age gives them no legal protection.", "The 2024 Act made polls binding; there's no requirement to restore wards.", "It's lawful if Parliament creates it; the issue is permanence."],why:"Lawful because Parliament allows them, and vulnerable for the same reason."},

// ---------- Urgency, entrenchment and law-making ----------
{id:"lm1",sp:"law-making",k:"tension",q:"What is the key point about controls on law-making?",a:"Mostly self-imposed: the House's own rules, run by the executive",w:["The Constitution Act 1986 sets out every stage of a Bill, so courts can enforce them", "The courts supervise the legislative process for fairness", "The Governor-General can return a Bill for further review"],x:["The stages are in Standing Orders, not the Constitution Act, and courts won't enforce them.", "Courts stay out of proceedings in Parliament (art 9).", "There's no such power; assent follows advice."],why:"Section 6 (ministers must be MPs) fuses executive and legislature; there is no upper house."},
{id:"lm2",sp:"law-making",k:"proof",q:"What does urgency do?",a:"Faster stages and longer sittings; it can even skip select committee",w:["Lets royal assent come before the third reading when Parliament is short of time", "Suspends the Attorney-General's s 7 reporting duty", "Requires 75% of the House before it can be used"],x:["Assent always follows third reading.", "Section 7 still applies.", "Urgency is a simple-majority motion."],why:"Select committee is the main check, and urgency can remove it."},
{id:"lm3",sp:"law-making",k:"proof",q:"Why can't a court fix an abuse of urgency?",a:"Courts won't question proceedings or look behind an Act",w:["Urgency is entrenched by Electoral Act s 268, so courts have no say", "NZBORA s 4 bars review of Standing Orders", "Only the Supreme Court may hear the claim"],x:["Urgency isn't in s 268.", "Section 4 is about inconsistent Acts, not Standing Orders.", "No court will hear it at all."],why:"Standing Orders can be suspended at will; the remedy is political."},
{id:"lm4",sp:"law-making",k:"proof",q:"Which recent Act shows a s 7 warning being overridden?",a:"The Electoral Amendment Act 2025",w:["The Parliament Act 2025", "The Constitution Amendment Act 2026", "The Regulatory Standards Act 2025"],x:["No s 7 override story; it restated privilege.", "Not the example; it's a narrow s 6 fix.", "Not the s 7 example."],why:"A s 7 report is a warning, not a veto."},
{id:"lm5",sp:"law-making",k:"proof",q:"Why is entrenchment weak in law?",a:"Section 268 is not itself reserved, so a two-step repeal is possible",w:["Courts have struck s 268 down as an attempt by one Parliament to bind the next", "It needs only 60% of the House, not 75%, so it is easily met", "It protects only the Treaty clauses, not the electoral rules"],x:["No court has struck s 268 down.", "Section 268 requires 75% or a poll.", "Section 268 protects electoral rules, not the Treaty."],why:"Manner and form theory (Trethowan, Westco) suggests courts might enforce a procedure, but none has."},
{id:"lm6",sp:"law-making",k:"proof",q:"Why did the 2022 water entrenchment cause an uproar?",a:"A bare majority added a 60% lock on policy, then dropped it",w:["It entrenched the Treaty of Waitangi in the water legislation", "A court struck it down as an unlawful attempt to bind Parliament", "It changed the term of Parliament without a referendum"],x:["It didn't entrench the Treaty.", "No court was involved; Parliament reversed it.", "Nothing to do with the term of Parliament."],why:"Entrenchment of ordinary policy by a bare majority binds successors without their consent."},
{id:"lm7",sp:"law-making",k:"proof",q:"What does the Regulatory Standards Act 2025 add?",a:"Principles and Bill assessment, but no rights and no effect on validity",w:["Courts may strike down Bills that fail its principles, once the Bill has passed", "It entrenches the rule of law against later repeal", "It replaces the Attorney-General's NZBORA s 7 report"],x:["Section 24: no effect on validity.", "Nothing is entrenched.", "Section 7 remains."],why:"Principles without teeth."},
{id:"lm8",sp:"law-making",k:"land",q:"Which conclusion lands best?",a:"Weak in law, real in politics: MMP bargaining and conventions",w:["The controls are strong, because courts enforce Standing Orders", "There are no real controls on Parliament at all", "Urgency becomes unlawful when it is used too often"],x:["Courts don't enforce Standing Orders.", "Too absolute: politics and conventions are real controls.", "Urgency is lawful however often it is used."],why:"Then ask whether a written constitution (Palmer and Butler) is worth the trade."}
];
